\description{
Fit one random-intercept mixed model per feature on a latent scale (such as the log-odds of a site's occupancy), where each sample is either measured on that scale or counted through it (k of n trials succeed), and test each named coefficient with a Wald t.

Answers \strong{does a value follow a trait, when some runs measured it and others only counted
successes, k of n?} A PTM site's occupancy is the usual case: where the modified and unmodified
peptides were both quantified you have a number; where they were not, you may know only that 3 of
8 covering peptidoforms carried the modification. \code{\link{stats_fit_mixed}} has to drop the counted
runs. This uses both kinds.
}
\details{
mzLib's \code{LogitNormalMixedModel.Fit} (mzLib #1469) is \code{\link{stats_fit_mixed}}'s model - fixed effects
from your design, one baseline per group drawn from N(0, tau^2), run-to-run scatter sigma^2 - on a
\strong{latent} value, such as the log-odds of a site's occupancy. A sample can be \strong{measured} (its
latent value is in \code{responses}) or \strong{counted}: k of its n trials succeeded (\code{counts}), each with
a chance g set by the latent value through the \code{link}, logit g = intercept + slope x latent +
depth_slope x depth. It is \code{lme4::glmer} (a binomial GLMM, with an observation-level random effect
for sigma^2) and \code{lmer} in one model, which mzLib checks against both and against \code{glmmTMB}.

\strong{Which cells are counted, and the link, are your choices}: mzLibR builds neither.
\code{\link{stats_fit_link}} fits a link from cells.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_latent_mixed_model_fit}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per (reported coefficient, feature) - one block of
    \code{feature_count} rows per coefficient you named, in that order, each in the feature order of
    the responses file (of the counts file, without one): \code{feature}; \code{coefficient}; \code{status}, one
    of \code{"fitted"}, \code{"too_few_observations"}, \code{"rank_deficient"}, \code{"separated"} (no finite
    estimate exists: a status, never a p-value), \code{"not_converged"} or \code{"too_few_groups"};
    \code{estimate}, the fixed effect within groups on the latent scale (latent units per coefficient
    unit: \strong{natural log-odds} for a logit latent, so \code{exp(estimate)} is an odds ratio);
    \code{standard_error} (latent units per coefficient unit), from the inverse Hessian at the
    estimated variances; \code{df} (degrees of freedom), nlme's containment df over the samples with a
    response of either kind, \code{NA} when not fitted or when no positive df exists; \code{t} (standard
    errors), the Wald statistic; \code{p_value}, a fraction (0 to 1), two-sided on \code{df}, \strong{not
    adjusted across features}, \code{NA} also when \code{df} is; \code{residual_variance} (squared latent
    units), sigma^2, run to run within a group, estimated or the one you gave;
    \code{residual_variance_given}, \code{TRUE} when it is the one you gave; \code{group_variance} (squared
    latent units), tau^2, where 0 is the boundary; \code{log_likelihood} (log-likelihood, natural log),
    Laplace-approximated restricted under \code{"reml"}, full under \code{"ml"}; \code{measured}, samples with a
    finite response (samples); \code{counted}, samples with a count (samples); and \code{groups}, distinct
    groups among both (groups). Statistics are \code{NA} where \code{status} is not \code{"fitted"}.
  - \code{responses_file}, \code{counts_file}, \code{depth_file} and \code{residual_variance_file} (\code{NA} when not
    given), \code{design_file} and \code{groups_file}, as absolute paths; \code{grouping}, the groups file's
    label column header; \code{group_names}, in order of first appearance; \code{group_count} groups;
    \code{feature_count} features and \code{sample_count} samples read; \code{sample_names};
    \code{coefficient_names} and \code{tested_coefficients}; \code{estimator}, the estimator mzLib used;
    \code{link}, the link every count was read through, as mzLib reports it: an
    \code{mzlibr_detection_link} with \code{intercept} (natural log-odds), \code{slope} (natural log-odds per
    latent unit), \code{depth_slope} (natural log-odds per depth unit) and \code{overdispersion} (fraction,
    the intra-cell correlation, 0 to below 1); \code{threads} (threads); \code{measured_cells},
    \code{counted_cells} and \code{missing_count} (cells); \code{status_counts}, features per status
    (features); \code{row_count} rows; \code{column_names}.
  - \code{caveats}: what the model is and is not, the link and the estimator used, plus counts for this
    input: unfitted features, rows with no df, features at the boundary, residual variances given.

}
\section{What it is not}{


A random intercept per group and nothing more: no random slopes, nested or crossed groupings.
Responses and estimates are on the latent scale, never fractions or intensities. Standard errors
are at the estimated variances and do not include their uncertainty, so they are not \code{glmmTMB}'s
\code{vcov()}. Under \code{"reml"} the fixed effects are integrated out by a Laplace approximation, as
\code{glmmTMB}'s \code{REML = TRUE} does; with nothing counted it is exactly \code{\link{stats_fit_mixed}}'s REML,
and the two agree to about 1e-5. Send one coefficient's \code{p_value} column to \code{\link{stats_adjust}} for
Benjamini-Hochberg.

}
