\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{responses}}{path; in \strong{latent units (e.g. natural log-odds of occupancy)}; default absent. The measured samples: stats fit-mixed's responses table, a TSV with a header row, the feature id first (unique) and then one column per sample, named by its header. Values are on the LATENT scale (logit occupancy, for example), not fractions or intensities. A blank cell, NA or NaN (any case) is not measured. At least one of responses and counts is required. Absent, no sample is measured: the bridge passes an all-missing matrix of the counts' shape.}
\item{\code{counts}}{path; in \strong{trials (successes of trials)}; default absent; range \code{each cell k/n, two whole numbers}. The counted samples (mzLib's BinomialCount): a TSV laid out as responses, each cell successes/trials such as 3/8, or blank, NA or NaN for a sample not counted. When both tables are given they must list exactly the same feature ids and sample columns, in any order (the responses' order is kept), and a cell may be measured or counted, not both (mzLib's rule). mzLib refuses a count with no trials, or more successes than trials. Absent, no sample is counted, and the model is stats fit-mixed's.}
\item{\code{design}}{path; required. stats fit's design table: one row per sample, the sample name (matching the fit's sample columns exactly, rows matched by name) then one column per fixed-effect coefficient, every cell a finite number. Include an intercept column if you want one; do not add a column per group, which the random intercept replaces. mzLib refuses a design that is not of full column rank over all samples.}
\item{\code{groups}}{path; required. stats fit-mixed's groups table: one row per sample, the sample name then EXACTLY ONE column, the sample's group label (an animal, a donor, a dataset), whose header names the grouping. The sample names must be exactly the fit's sample columns. Labels are trimmed and compared exactly; mzLib refuses an empty one.}
\item{\code{link}}{string; in \strong{natural log-odds}; required; range \code{identity, or 2 to 4 comma-separated numbers: intercept,slope[,depth_slope[,overdispersion]]}. mzLib's DetectionLink: how the latent value sets the chance g that one trial of a counted cell succeeds, logit g = intercept + slope x latent + depth_slope x depth, with trials in a cell correlated by overdispersion (rho, a beta-binomial; 0 is the binomial). REQUIRED: mzLib's link parameter has no default. identity is DetectionLink.Identity, (0, 1): a counted cell then samples the same probability a measured one reads. Numbers are passed as the record's positional arguments, so a depth_slope or overdispersion left out is the record's own default (0 at cc67b7a2). stats fit-link fits one from cells. mzLib refuses a non-finite intercept or depth slope, a slope that is 0 or not finite, and an overdispersion outside [0, 1).}
\item{\code{depth}}{path; in \strong{depth units (the caller's, e.g. log2 target PSMs)}; default absent. A TSV with a header row and one row per sample: the sample name (exactly the fit's samples, in any order), then one number, the sample's depth for the link's depth term. Needed when the link's depth_slope is not 0 (mzLib refuses otherwise). Absent, the bridge passes none.}
\item{\code{residual_variance} (wire \code{--residual-variance})}{path; in \strong{squared latent units}; default absent; range \code{each > 0 and finite, or blank to estimate}. A TSV with a header row and one row per feature: the feature id, then sigma^2 to use for it instead of estimating it (e.g. borrowed when a feature has too few measured samples). A blank, NA or NaN cell, or a feature not listed, is NaN, which mzLib reads as estimate it. An id that is not one of the fit's features is refused. Absent, the bridge passes none and every sigma^2 is estimated.}
\item{\code{coefficients} (wire \code{--stdin})}{string[]; required; range \code{>= 1 line; each a design column name, none repeated}. The coefficients to report, one design column name per line. The model always fits every design column; the table holds one block of rows per coefficient named here, in this order.}
\item{\code{estimator}}{string; default absent; range \code{reml | ml}. reml is VarianceEstimator.Reml (the fixed effects integrated out by a Laplace approximation, as glmmTMB's REML; exact when nothing is counted), ml is VarianceEstimator.MaximumLikelihood. Absent, the bridge sends none and LogitNormalMixedModel.Fit's default applies (Reml at cc67b7a2); the envelope's estimator says which was used.}
\item{\code{threads}}{int; in \strong{threads}; default \code{1}; range \code{>= 1, or -1 for every core}. Features fitted at once. The bridge default is 1 (BULK.md); mzLib's own default is -1. Results are identical at any value (mzLib).}
}
}
