\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item A random intercept per group on a latent scale: a measured sample reads the latent; a counted sample has k of n trials succeed with logit g = intercept + slope x latent + depth_slope x depth, binomially or beta-binomially. No random slopes, nested or crossed groupings.
\item Responses and estimates are on the latent scale (natural log-odds for a logit latent), not fractions or intensities. Which cells are counted, and the link, are the caller's modelling choices; the bridge builds neither (stats fit-link fits a link).
\item Counted samples are integrated by adaptive Gauss-Hermite quadrature (40 nodes over the group intercept, 12 over each count's residual), measured ones exactly. mzLib agrees with lme4's glmer (nAGQ 25, ML) to about 1e-6, with lmer to about 1e-5 when nothing is counted, and with glmmTMB to its Laplace error (about 1\% in tau^2 and standard errors, 5\% in sigma^2) when sigma^2 is estimated from counts alone (ReferenceComparisonLogitNormalTests).
\item Fixed effects are tested by Wald t on nlme's containment df, over samples with a response of either kind. standard_error is at the estimated variances and does not include their uncertainty, so it is not glmmTMB's vcov().
\item Under reml the fixed effects are integrated out by a Laplace approximation (glmmTMB's REML = TRUE); it is exact when nothing is counted. Under REML glmmTMB reports beta as the joint mode of (beta, u), so compare its estimates with this verb's ml ones only.
\item With nothing counted the model is stats fit-mixed's, fitted by a different optimiser: under reml the numbers agree with fit-mixed's to about 1e-5; under ml estimates and variances agree, but standard errors are at the ML variances, where fit-mixed's scale by RSS / (observed - coefficients) as nlme's summary does.
\item separated is a status, never a p-value: the counts are (quasi-)completely separated by the design and the estimate runs to infinity.
\item p_value is not adjusted across features. Send one coefficient's p_value column to stats adjust for Benjamini-Hochberg.
}
}
\section{Performance}{
Every call starts one bridge process, which costs a .NET start-up before any work. 
}
